WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Nowadays agents get a lot of attention, but for a wide class of problems a well-designed workflow

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.239
is cheaper, faster, and easier to maintain. So when do deterministic workflows actually

3
00:00:12.239 --> 00:00:18.559
outperform agentic systems and why? Over the next few minutes we'll walk through the five

4
00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:24.159
core workflow patterns, where each one fits, and how to make that call confidently.

5
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.879
The whole workflow versus agent question comes down to one thing. Who decides the control flow?

6
00:00:30.879 --> 00:00:36.799
In a deterministic workflow, you do. It's a fixed, developer-authored sequence of steps,

7
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:42.240
with branches and loops all specified at design time. Nothing surprises you at runtime,

8
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.319
because you wrote every decision in advance. In an agentic system, the model decides. It

9
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:53.520
picks which actions to take, and in what order, to pursue the goal you handed it.

10
00:00:53.599 --> 00:00:58.240
You don't fully control that sequence. Prompt chaining sits at the simple end of the workflow

11
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.959
side, passing one call's output directly as the input to the next. It's linear, testable,

12
00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:10.080
and easy to debug. Hold on to that distinction, because everything else in this lesson depends on

13
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:17.519
it. The core trade-off is straightforward. Every architecture decision comes down to one question.

14
00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:23.760
Does this task require runtime judgment? Workflows trade adaptability for predictability.

15
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.800
You know exactly what will happen because you authored every step. Agents flip that,

16
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.720
giving you open-ended flexibility at the cost of predictability and debugability. Here's what

17
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.239
practitioners often miss. Most real tasks fall much closer to the workflow end of that spectrum.

18
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.480
The appeal of agents is real, but choosing them when a workflow would work adds token cost and

19
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.519
operational risk without adding meaningful capability. That's a trade-off worth being

20
00:01:51.519 --> 00:01:58.080
deliberate about every single time you design a system. Let's put workflows and agents side by

21
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:05.360
side. Workflows have fixed control flow, predictable output, auditable steps, lower latency, and lower

22
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:12.320
token cost. Every step is individually testable, and failures localize cleanly to a specific

23
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.800
location. Agents give you dynamic control flow and adaptive behavior. The capability ceiling

24
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.279
is higher, but so is the cost, the latency, and the failure surface. Debugging an agentic reasoning

25
00:02:25.279 --> 00:02:31.039
trace is significantly harder than reading a structured workflow execution log. For any given

26
00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:37.440
task, ask yourself which set of properties you actually need, not which architecture sounds more

27
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:44.639
capable or technically impressive to your team. Before reaching for an agent, run through three

28
00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:50.639
quick checks. First, is the input format predictable and well-defined at the time you build the system?

29
00:02:51.279 --> 00:02:57.919
Second, is the output format known and testable against a clear specification? Third, can every

30
00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:04.000
processing step be specified at design time without requiring runtime judgment from the model?

31
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.520
When all three are true, a workflow is the right answer. You're not leaving capability on the table

32
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.039
by choosing a workflow. You're making a deliberate engineering choice that gives you lower cost,

33
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:21.279
faster execution, and a system your team can actually reason about and fix confidently when

34
00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:28.160
something inevitably goes wrong in production. Anthropx documentation defines five core workflow

35
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:35.600
patterns. Prompt chaining, sequential LLM calls where each output feeds the next step. Routing,

36
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.320
a classifier step directs input to the right specialized prompt or sub-agent. Parallelization,

37
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.360
multiple LLM calls run simultaneously with results collected at a fan-in step. Orchestrator workers,

38
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:56.240
a controlling component decomposes a goal and delegates to specialized workers. And evaluator

39
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.800
optimizer, one call generates output, another evaluates it, looping until quality criteria are

40
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:09.360
met. Each pattern solves a distinct structural problem and each one remains a workflow. None of

41
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:16.880
them require the full autonomy and unpredictability that define a true agentic system. Routing and

42
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.640
parallelization are worth a closer look because they handle real complexity without requiring

43
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.279
agentic autonomy. A routing workflow uses a classifier, sometimes an LLM, sometimes a simple

44
00:04:29.279 --> 00:04:35.279
rule to sort inputs before processing them. The topology is fixed even when the router itself

45
00:04:35.279 --> 00:04:41.760
calls a language model. Parallelization fans work out across multiple concurrent calls and collects

46
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.959
results at a synchronization point before proceeding. Both patterns scale to complex

47
00:04:46.959 --> 00:04:52.000
workloads and production traffic volumes. And because the control flow is still authored at

48
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.000
design time, both remain fully auditable. You get sophisticated complexity handling without

49
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.399
giving up the predictability that makes systems reliable and maintainable. Let's be precise about

50
00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:10.640
when not to over-index on agents. Agent overhead, which includes the extra token spend, the dynamic

51
00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.720
planning loops, and the harder-to-audit execution traces, is only justified when the task genuinely

52
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:23.119
can't be prescripted. Agents increase token spend on every single task run, not just occasionally.

53
00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:29.440
Every autonomous decision the LLM makes is a potential failure point you didn't explicitly

54
00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.920
design for or anticipate. And debugging agentic traces takes significantly more engineering time

55
00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.160
than reading a structured workflow execution log. There's a reliability angle too. A deterministic

56
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:48.000
step is individually testable, so a workflow failure localizes to one identifiable step.

57
00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:54.480
In an agentic trace, the root cause can be buried anywhere in a long reasoning chain. The principle

58
00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.720
is simple. Reach for agents only when workflows demonstrably fall short. That's disciplined

59
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:08.720
engineering, not a limitation. The whole question here is who authors the control flow? You at design

60
00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:15.600
time or the model during runtime? That matters because agent overhead is real, and you pay it

61
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.399
every run whether the task needed that judgment or not. One thing to watch. Capability that sounds

62
00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:29.040
impressive isn't the same as capability the task actually needs. That closes out this lesson.

63
00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.399
I'll see you in the next one.

